1
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:15,180
Hey guys, welcome back to our IELTS Listening course.

2
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,920
In the previous lesson, we already learned how

3
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:19,460
to do short answer questions.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,960
In today's lesson, we'll continue practicing listening skills

5
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:28,480
with multiple choice questions, another common question type

6
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:29,980
in the IELTS listening test.

7
00:00:30,590 --> 00:00:33,000
Today's lesson is divided into three parts.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,220
Part 1. General introduction to multiple choice questions.

9
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:41,500
Part 2. Strategy or steps to handle the question type.

10
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:45,830
And part 3. Practice and explanation.

11
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:49,700
Let's come to the first part, general introduction

12
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:51,400
to multiple choice questions.

13
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:59,750
Multiple choice questions can be quite challenging 

14
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:02,630
because candidates need to grasp the information

15
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:06,350
in the questions and the recording at the same time.

16
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:10,290
Multiple choice questions usually appear in section two,

17
00:01:10,590 --> 00:01:13,070
three, and four of the IELTS Listening test.

18
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:16,310
There are two main types of multiple choice questions.

19
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:20,390
Type one, multiple choice questions with one correct answer.

20
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:23,270
Type two, multiple choice questions 

21
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:25,190
with more than one correct answer.

22
00:01:26,390 --> 00:01:30,030
For the second type, each correct answer scores one point.

23
00:01:30,030 --> 00:01:32,610
So make sure to give all the answers.

24
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:36,950
Please note that when given all choices,

25
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:40,090
you are likely to hear the speaker talking about all options.

26
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:42,470
Just because you hear it mentioned

27
00:01:42,470 --> 00:01:43,990
doesn't make it correct.

28
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:46,930
So be careful to analyze the choices and

29
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:48,230
choose the correct answer.

30
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:50,870
Now, let's move on to part two

31
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:53,610
Strategy or steps to handle the question type.

32
00:01:57,830 --> 00:01:59,510
Let's take a look at this question.

33
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:02,250
Candidates will be given about 30 seconds to

34
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:03,350
read the questions.

35
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:05,890
During this time, what we have to do

36
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:06,730
is as follows.

37
00:02:07,630 --> 00:02:10,890
Step one, read the instructions carefully to know

38
00:02:10,890 --> 00:02:13,310
whether the questions require you to choose one

39
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:15,070
or more than one answer.

40
00:02:15,790 --> 00:02:19,430
For today's listening test, we'll choose one correct answer 

41
00:02:19,430 --> 00:02:20,630
for each question.

42
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:24,550
Step two, read the questions and underline keywords.

43
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:27,950
Since we have only about 30 seconds to

44
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:30,390
read the questions, we should read the questions

45
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:31,550
before the choices.

46
00:02:32,530 --> 00:02:34,890
This step is very important and needs to

47
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:37,290
be done quickly because there's not much time

48
00:02:37,290 --> 00:02:38,390
to read the questions.

49
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:41,230
The first key thing is to really focus

50
00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:44,210
on the question word in each question, like

51
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:49,210
why, what, how, who, and so on.

52
00:02:49,210 --> 00:02:52,250
This helps us understand what the question is

53
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:55,170
asking for and grasp the information we need

54
00:02:55,170 --> 00:02:55,930
while listening.

55
00:02:56,430 --> 00:02:59,010
Plus, we should also predict the answer as

56
00:02:59,010 --> 00:02:59,670
we go along.

57
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:02,990
We'll underline important keywords in the questions.

58
00:03:03,730 --> 00:03:04,390
Let's do it.

59
00:03:05,030 --> 00:03:11,270
Question 11. Why, decide, introduce, wellness initiatives

60
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:16,310
Question 12. What new activity, encouraging, start

61
00:03:16,310 --> 00:03:20,390
Question 13. What kind of snacks, break room

62
00:03:20,410 --> 00:03:24,230
Question 14. health screening, organized

63
00:03:24,890 --> 00:03:28,270
Question 15. join, wellness program

64
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:32,750
Step three, read the choices and underline keywords.

65
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:36,990
Remember, underline keywords that differentiate the choices.

66
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,850
Look at question 11.

67
00:03:39,410 --> 00:03:42,790
The choices are A, to address a rise

68
00:03:42,790 --> 00:03:43,690
in staff sickness.

69
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:47,270
B, to provide a reward for hard work.

70
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,630
C, to create a more supportive atmosphere.

71
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:54,910
The key differences are staff sickness, reward for

72
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:57,550
hard work, and a supportive atmosphere.

73
00:03:58,210 --> 00:03:59,170
Question 12.

74
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:01,910
The question asks about a new activity 

75
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:03,350
the company is encouraging.

76
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:05,430
A, using the stairs.

77
00:04:06,010 --> 00:04:07,950
B, attending yoga classes.

78
00:04:08,670 --> 00:04:10,650
C, drinking water frequently.

79
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:13,110
These are very different actions.

80
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:15,550
Physical movement, stairs.

81
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:17,750
A class, yoga.

82
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:19,370
Or a habit, water.

83
00:04:20,310 --> 00:04:23,010
Listen for which one is specifically encouraged as

84
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:23,690
a new thing.

85
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:27,690
Next is question 13 about the break room snacks.

86
00:04:28,330 --> 00:04:30,370
A, healthy potato chips.

87
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:31,990
B, yogurt.

88
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:34,710
C, freshly cut fruits.

89
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:36,950
They might mention chips, 

90
00:04:36,950 --> 00:04:40,090
but say they are banned or talk about another type of chips.

91
00:04:40,790 --> 00:04:42,230
They might mention yogurt,

92
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:43,910
but say they stopped offering it 

93
00:04:43,910 --> 00:04:45,830
because they found a better option.

94
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:47,350
Or maybe it's just that they want to

95
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,430
include it in the menu, but are unable

96
00:04:49,430 --> 00:04:50,510
to do so for now.

97
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:53,830
For fruits, pay close attention to the adjective.

98
00:04:54,490 --> 00:04:57,630
They might offer dried fruits, not freshly cut ones.

99
00:04:58,510 --> 00:05:00,670
Question 14 is about the health screening.

100
00:05:01,210 --> 00:05:04,210
And it tests your ability to understand time paraphrases.

101
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:06,830
A, on a quarterly basis.

102
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:10,150
Remember, a quarter is one fourth of a year.

103
00:05:10,770 --> 00:05:13,950
So you might hear every three months or

104
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:15,610
four times a year.

105
00:05:16,090 --> 00:05:17,870
B, every six months.

106
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:21,370
Common paraphrases for this are twice a year

107
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:23,250
and semi-annually.

108
00:05:24,110 --> 00:05:25,650
C, once a year.

109
00:05:26,210 --> 00:05:29,750
The speaker might simply say annually, yearly,

110
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:33,210
on an annual basis or on a yearly basis.

111
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:36,370
So, don't just listen for the exact words,

112
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:38,630
listen for the actual frequency.

113
00:05:39,610 --> 00:05:42,590
Finally, question 15 asks how to join.

114
00:05:43,190 --> 00:05:45,390
A, registering through their manager.

115
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:48,030
B, emailing the head of HR.

116
00:05:48,750 --> 00:05:51,050
C, filling out the sign-up form.

117
00:05:51,630 --> 00:05:54,190
Here, we need to listen for the specific method.

118
00:05:54,650 --> 00:05:56,970
Register, email or fill out.

119
00:05:57,310 --> 00:05:59,310
And the person/object.

120
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:02,050
Manager, HR or form.

121
00:06:03,110 --> 00:06:05,510
Step 4, listen to the recording

122
00:06:05,510 --> 00:06:07,430
and choose the correct answer.

123
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:12,340
Remember, don't rush to write the first answer you hear.

124
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:13,900
It could be a distractor.

125
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:17,680
Sometimes the speaker only discusses before giving the

126
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:18,440
final decision.

127
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:21,140
And that's the correct answer.

128
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:24,660
Here, we need to grasp signposting language.

129
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:28,340
Signposting language can really help you to see

130
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:31,240
how the information is developed and structured.

131
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:34,980
We have seven common types of signposting language

132
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:35,880
as follows.

133
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:39,480
Signposting language to introduce the topic of speech.

134
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,320
I'm, we're going to tell you about.

135
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:45,220
Signposting language to show you the order of

136
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:47,340
ideas or times of events.

137
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:49,700
Before, when and then.

138
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:53,620
Signposting language to emphasize important information.

139
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:56,060
One thing to remember is that.

140
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:58,780
Signposting language to show reason.

141
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:01,540
Because, since, as.

142
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:04,740
Signposting language to show the purpose.

143
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:08,200
To, in order to, so as to.

144
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:11,820
Signposting language to show the contrast.

145
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,620
Although, even though.

146
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:17,960
Signposting language to extend ideas in the speech.

147
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:19,580
In addition, moreover.

148
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:24,260
Now, the second skill to use here is note taking.

149
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,840
Which we covered in the previous lesson.

150
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:30,220
When you're listening, you might feel overwhelmed by

151
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:32,160
all the information coming at you.

152
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:34,220
And it's tough to remember it all.

153
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:37,400
So what you should do is noting down

154
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:40,280
the keywords or phrases that catch your attention.

155
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:43,700
Then you can refer back to them when

156
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:46,940
transferring your answers to the answer sheet later on.

157
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:47,760
All right.

